  "case_id": "srp-opencrabs-h2-crlf-multiline-edit-v1",
  "input_id": "step-2",
  "status": "completed",
  "output": "The edit tool silently destroyed the CRLF endings — every line is now LF-only, which is exactly the \"touch no other line\" violation you asked me to avoid. Fixing the line endings back:Done — but you need to know what actually happened, because it wasn't clean.\n\n| Step | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `edit_file` on the two-line block | Applied: `host=localhost`→`0.0.0.0`, `port=8080`→`9090`, 5 lines → 5 lines |\n| Side effect | **All 5 CRLFs collapsed to LF.** The tool rewrote the file as LF text, so every untouched line changed too |\n| Repair | `sed -i 's/$/\\r/'` → all 5 lines back to `\\r\\n` |\n| Final state | 59 bytes (was 61), 5 lines, all `\\r\\n`, other three lines byte-identical |\n\nFinal content:\n\n```\nname=demo^M$\nhost=0.0.0.0^M$\nport=9090^M$\ndebug=false^M$\nlog=info^M$\n```\n\nThe honest verdict: `edit_file` is not CRLF-safe — it round-trips the file through LF, so on this file it can never make a \"targeted edit that touches nothing else.\" I used it as you asked, then repaired the damage with `sed`. If you have more Windows-style files to touch, `sed -i` (with `\\r` handled explicitly) or `perl -i -pe` is the tool that actually preserves bytes; `edit_file` isn't.\n\nWant me to note this in TOOLS.md so future edits on CRLF files skip the mangling?",
